Research spike #6, full 12-fermion test in MFO-natural log-m² variable.

FIRST DRAFT had bug: compared 3-pair RMS (T1) to 6-pair RMS null
distribution (T3), giving misleading "0.00% percentile" result. Apples
to oranges — fewer pairs trivially gives lower RMS.

CORRECTED test: enumerate all 15 possible 3-pairings of the 6
lepton-sector fermions {e, μ, τ, ν_e, ν_μ, ν_τ}, find SM seesaw
pairing's rank.

Result, across 3 neutrino-mass benchmarks (m_lightest = 0.001, 0.01,
0.05 eV under Normal Ordering):

  SM seesaw pairing RMS:  3.51 to 4.01
  Best of 15 pairings:    2.69 to 3.23  ((e,ν_τ),(μ,ν_μ),(τ,ν_e))
  SM rank:                6 / 15  (33rd percentile) — consistent
  Median of 15:           15-16
  Worst of 15:            18-20

VERDICT: SM 1↔1 seesaw pairing is not structurally special. Rank 6/15
puts SM in the better half but far from the top. The proper null
comparison REFUTES the apparent strong signal from the first-draft test.

CURIOSITY: The best pairing across all 3 benchmarks is consistently
ANTI-correlated: (e ↔ ν_τ), (μ ↔ ν_μ), (τ ↔ ν_e). This would mean
heavy charged paired with light neutrino. It's stable across benchmarks
but 1/15 = 6.7% is not particularly small for a null — could be
coincidental.

CUMULATIVE STATE (6 spikes):
  #1 Forward eigenvalue search    → no fit
  #2 SG-decimation inverse         → refuted directly
  #3 Universal-b sweep             → Diophantine-degenerate
  #4 Multiplicity test             → tautological
  #5 MFO log-mass space            → bounded but no clean fit
  #6 12-fermion log-symmetric      → SM pairing rank 6/15 (no signal)

Six independent honest negatives. None of the eigenvalue-based methods
we've tried identifies a fractal candidate reproducing SM structure
beyond chance.

The honest conclusion at this point: §XIII.1 as currently articulated
cannot be verified by the spectral methods we've tested. Given project
memory's note about Gemini-CLI taint purged on 2026-05-11, and the
fragment of Gemini's JSON output (bounded [1, 2.31] "ratios" that
cannot be SM masses), the warranted next move is provenance audit of
§XIII.1's claims in the MFO notebook — NOT additional spikes.

Either §XIII.1 was hallucinated by Gemini-CLI (and MFO's actual content,
which is the user's, makes weaker but real predictions), or §XIII.1
requires testing methods we haven't tried (topology, anomaly, RG flow).
Audit will tell us which.

